+# UI text-fit gate
+
+Owner rule (2026-11-15): all texts are formatted and fitted into place, without clipping or blocking UI.
+The gate is a unit test that runs in `make -C core test`. It draws nothing: it measures. Plan: `docs/design/UI_PLAN.md`
+section 3b. Stream: UI.
+
+## What it checks
+Every registered screen is run at 240x160 and at 320x240, in English and in every ready language pack
+(`doomo_lang_info[].ready`). For every string the screen lists, with its box and the things next to it:
+
+1. **Overflow.** A one-line text wider than its box; a wrapped text with more lines than its box has;
+   a text that is off the screen.
+2. **Cut word.** A wrapped text that must break inside a word (a word wider than the box); a hint bar that is
+   still too wide after the draw code's own squeeze (`ui_hint`: one space between items, then whole words go,
+   that is a cut); a text that the draw code cuts with "..." (the pause menu labels).
+3. **Cover.** The drawn rectangle of a text intersects a block (button, cursor, arrow, portrait) or another text
+   of the same state.
+
+The measuring uses the core's own functions, which every port shares: `ui_width_raw` (the width `ui_text_width`
+gives), the wrap rule of `ui_text_wrap` (a self-test keeps the gate's wrapper and `ui_text_wrap` in step), and
+`lang_tr` plus the piece translation of `text_draw` (runs between the A / B button icons of a text that has no
+translation of its own). A text is measured as drawn: translated.
+
+## Running it
+- `make -C core -j2 test` runs it (tests `textfit_gate_every_shared_screen_fits_in_every_language`,
+  `textfit_engine_*`, `textfit_wrap_matches_ui_text_wrap`, `textfit_scan_finds_the_hints_prompts_and_buttons`).
+- Only the gate: `core/build/tests --golden core/tests/golden --tmp core/build/testtmp textfit_gate`.
+- A failure prints one line per hit: where it happens (`@240: en es @320: en`), the screen, its state, the
+  item, what is wrong, the string. Fix the string (shorter, in English or in the pack), wrap it, or move the rect.
+  Never raise a box to hide a hit that a reader would see on the real screen.
+
+## Screens that are on the gate
+| Screen | Where it is listed | What it covers |
+|---|---|---|
+| `options` | `fp_options_textfit` in `fp_options.c` | every row label with every value and its arrows, every help text (all pads, presets, unlock hints, reset rows) in the help pill, the page tab strip at every page, the hint bars |
+| `shop` | `fp_shop_textfit` in `fp_shop.c` | the tab strips of the three counters, the counter texts (Coming soon!, unlock text, item description at 320x240) |
+| `title` | `fp_title_textfit` in `fp_title.c` | main buttons, Language and Theme rows with arrows, submenu buttons, the one-line help |
+| `pause` | `textfit_screens.c` | the labels of the shared pause menu, its title and hints |
+| `scanned_hints_prompts_buttons` | `textfit_screens.c` | a scan of every `core/src/*.c`: each literal passed to `ui_hint`, `ui_confirm` (window and Yes / No buttons), `ui_title`, and `ui_button(x, y, <number>, "label")` is measured in its real box. A new hint or prompt is covered the day it is written |
+
+A screen that is only drawn on one size is listed in `big_only` of `textfit_screens.c` (hints behind `big &&`).
+Toasts scroll when they are too wide (`ui.c`, by design), item names scroll in a marquee: neither is measured.
+
+## Adding a screen (TALK, CREATOR, STUDIO, LEVELS, DC, any stream)
+The API is `core/include/doomo/textfit.h`. A screen is a function that lists strings, boxes and blocks; it draws
+nothing. Put it in a test file of your own (`core/tests/textfit_<stream>.c`) or next to your draw code under
+`#if defined(DOOMO_TEXTFIT) && DOOMO_TEXTFIT` (the native core build defines it, the consoles do not: no byte in
+any ROM or EXE), then register it:
+
+```c
+static void talk_screen(TfScreen *s)
+{
+    tf_state(s, "answer list");                                  /* things on screen together */
+    tf_block(s, "portrait", 4, 100, 40, 40);                     /* something text must not cover */
+    tf_text(s, "answer 1", "Tell me more", 48, 100, tf_sw(s) - 56, 1, TF_ONE);
+    tf_text(s, "question", question_text, 48, 60, tf_sw(s) - 56, 3, TF_WRAP);
+    tf_fit(s, "tab strip", strip_width, tf_sw(s) - 10);          /* a whole that must fit */
+}
+TEXTFIT_SCREEN(talk, talk_screen)
+```
+- `tf_sw`, `tf_sh`, `tf_big`, `tf_lang` give the size and language of this run.
+- Pass the English literal; the gate translates it. Texts built at run time are passed built, with `TF_RAW`, in
+  their longest form (a number of digits, the longest name from the table).
+- Flags: `TF_ONE` (default, one line), `TF_WRAP` (word wrap, `maxlines`), `TF_RIGHT`, `TF_CENTER`, `TF_RAW`,
+  `TF_BAR` (a prompt bar: the draw code's squeeze applies).
+- One `tf_state` per set of things that are on screen at the same time; texts of different states are never
+  compared. Use one state per row or per page for a table.
+- Use the same geometry constants as the draw code (copy the numbers next to the draw code, or export a small
+  rect function and call it from both). A box that is looser than the draw code hides hits.
+- A new gate screen must be green before the package lands.
+
+## History
+- UI-FIT (2026-11-15): the gate; first hit list 24 (see `docs/design/held/status/UI.md`), all fixed.
+  Old per-screen checks (`fp_*_overflow` of CL5) stay; the gate is their superset for shared screens.
